export const TOPICS = [
{code:"1.1",theme:1,title:"Population dynamics",
 outcomes:["Describe and give reasons for the rapid increase in world population","Explain over-population and under-population","Understand the main causes of a change in population size","Give reasons for contrasting rates of natural population change","Describe and evaluate population policies"],
 guidance:["Causes and consequences of over- and under-population","How birth rate, death rate and migration make a population grow or decline","Social, economic and other factors — including government policy and HIV/AIDS — on birth and death rates"],
 cases:[{t:"A country which is over-populated",src:"India",notes:["india-an-overpopulated-country"]},{t:"A country which is under-populated",src:"Australia",notes:["australia-population-density-and-distribution"]},{t:"A country with a high rate of natural population growth",src:"Uganda",notes:["uganda-a-youthful-population"]},{t:"A country with a low rate of growth or decline",src:"Japan",notes:["japan-a-declining-and-ageing-population"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Sort every factor into political, social, economic, demographic — that structure is worth marks on its own.","Always run the chain: factor → what it changes → effect on birth or death rate → effect on population.","Learn the five DTM stages with one named country each, and what causes each transition.","For policies, know the aim, two measures and one unintended consequence."],
   q:[["Explain why birth rates are falling in many more economically developed countries.",5,
        ["Women stay longer in education and build careers, so they marry and start families later — fewer years to have children.","Contraception and family planning are cheap, available and socially accepted.","Children are expensive (housing, education, childcare) and are not needed to work for the family.","Low infant mortality means parents no longer have extra children in case some die.","Pensions and state care mean parents don't rely on children to support them in old age.","Each reason needs the mechanism — \"education\" on its own scores little; say what it changes about family size."]],
      ["Describe the consequences of over-population for a country.",4,
        ["Shortages of food and clean water, leading to malnutrition and disease.","Not enough housing, so overcrowding and squatter settlements grow.","Services overloaded — crowded schools and hospitals, too few teachers and doctors.","Not enough jobs: unemployment, underemployment and poverty.","Environmental damage — deforestation, overgrazing, soil erosion, pollution.","Describe consequences for the country, not causes of over-population."]],
      ["Using a named example, evaluate a population policy.",7,
        ["Name it with dates: China's one-child policy, introduced 1979 (population 969 million, fertility rate 2.74), ended 2015.","Aim: slow population growth to avoid economic collapse and raise living standards.","Incentives: extra pension, access to government jobs, free housing and land, priority healthcare, extra exam points for the child.","Penalties: heavy fines, forced sterilisation and abortion (108 million sterilisations 1980–2015), unregistered children denied education and jobs.","Success: growth slowed sharply — fertility fell well below replacement (about 1.16 births per woman today).","Problems: gender imbalance (about 30 million more males than females), ageing population, shrinking workforce, human-rights abuses.","Finish with a judgement — e.g. effective at cutting growth, but at a high social cost, which is why it was relaxed.","7 marks are level-marked: Level 3 (6–7) needs developed points with place-specific detail and an evaluation."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask them to sketch the DTM from memory and label two real countries onto it. Whatever they can't place is the session.",
   check:["They separate natural increase from total population change (migration is the difference).","They can explain <em>why</em> death rate falls before birth rate, not just that it does.","Their policy example has figures — birth rate before and after."],
   slip:"Listing factors as bare nouns (\"education\", \"religion\") with no mechanism. Push for the sentence after the noun."}},

{code:"1.2",theme:1,title:"Migration",
 outcomes:["Explain and give reasons for population migration","Demonstrate an understanding of the impacts of migration"],
 guidance:["Internal movements such as rural-urban migration, and international migration, both voluntary and involuntary","Positive and negative impacts on the destination, on the origin, and on the migrants themselves"],
 cases:[{t:"An international migration",src:"Mexico → USA",notes:["mexico-to-usa-international-migration"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Keep push and pull factors on opposite sides of a page and make each one a full clause.","Learn impacts in a 3×2 grid: origin / destination / migrants, each with positive and negative.","Know the difference between voluntary and forced migration and one example of each.","Carry three figures for your named migration — numbers, remittances, a date."],
   q:[["Describe the difference between voluntary and forced migration.",3,
        ["Voluntary migration: people choose to move, usually for better jobs, wages, education or quality of life — e.g. Mexico to the USA.","Forced migration: people have no choice because of war, persecution or natural disaster — e.g. refugees fleeing conflict.","The key difference is choice; an example of each secures the third mark."]],
      ["Explain the impacts of international migration on the destination country.",6,
        ["Positive: migrants fill labour shortages in farming, construction, care and healthcare.","Positive: they pay taxes and spend money, boosting the economy; young migrants offset an ageing population.","Positive: cultural diversity — food, music, festivals.","Negative: pressure on housing, schools and healthcare in areas where migrants settle.","Negative: competition for low-paid jobs may hold down wages; tension and discrimination can follow.","Cover both positives and negatives, and develop each point (\"…so…\") — impacts on the destination only."]],
      ["For a named international migration, describe the reasons why people moved.",7,
        ["Name it: Mexico to the USA, with figures — the USA attracts around 2.6 million migrants a year.","Economic pull: jobs and higher wages in a stable MEDC economy; migrants send money home (remittances) to support family.","Social pull: better healthcare and education — US literacy 99%, 3.6 doctors per 1,000; HDI 0.9 vs Mexico's 0.78.","Political pull: greater stability and safety in the USA.","Push factors in Mexico: lower wages, unemployment, poorer services, crime and violence.","Organise as push and pull, and make every factor specific to Mexico or the USA.","7 marks are level-marked: Level 3 (6–7) needs several developed reasons with place-specific detail."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"\"Give me your named migration in 60 seconds — from where, to where, how many, when, why.\" Time it.",
   check:["Impacts cover the source country, not just the receiving one.","They mention impacts on the migrants themselves — the most commonly forgotten third.","Push factors are pressures at origin, not attractions elsewhere."],
   slip:"Treating all migration as economic. Ask where conflict, drought and family reunification would sit."}},

{code:"1.3",theme:1,title:"Population structure",
 outcomes:["Identify and give reasons for, and implications of, different types of population structure"],
 guidance:["Age/sex pyramids of countries at different levels of economic development"],
 cases:[{t:"A country with a high dependent population",src:"Uganda / Japan",notes:["uganda-a-youthful-population","japan-a-declining-and-ageing-population"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Describe a pyramid in this order: base, sides, apex, then any dents or bulges — and quote figures off the axis.","Link shape to DTM stage and to a level of development every time.","Know both kinds of dependency — youthful and ageing — and the different problems each creates.","Practise reading percentages for named age bands rather than saying \"lots\"."],
   q:[["Describe the shape of the population pyramid shown.",4,
        ["Work in order: base (wide or narrow?), sides (taper quickly or stay straight?), apex (narrow or wide?).","Quote figures read off the axis — e.g. \"about 8% of males are aged 0–4\" — not \"lots of young people\".","Point out any bulge or indent and name the age band it affects.","Mention differences between males and females if they are visible.","Describe only — no reasons are needed for \"describe\"."]],
      ["Explain the problems caused by a high proportion of young dependants.",5,
        ["Governments must spend heavily on schools, teachers and child healthcare such as vaccinations.","A high dependency ratio: relatively few workers pay the taxes that support many children.","When they reach working age there may not be enough jobs — unemployment and migration to cities.","More demand for food and housing, leading to shortages and overcrowding.","Large numbers will soon reach child-bearing age, so the population keeps growing quickly (momentum).","Develop each problem: what is needed → why that is difficult for the country."]],
      ["Suggest reasons for the differences between the two pyramids.",6,
        ["Wide vs narrow base: high birth rate in the less developed country (little contraception, children as workers and security, high infant mortality, cultural or religious reasons) vs a low birth rate in the more developed one.","Steeply tapering vs straight sides: higher death rates and lower life expectancy due to poorer healthcare, diet and sanitation.","Narrow vs wide apex: few elderly vs a large elderly population living longer thanks to good healthcare.","Explain bulges or indents: baby boom, war, epidemic, population policy.","Link each difference you see to a reason — \"suggest\" means several valid explanations are accepted."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Show any two pyramids side by side with the labels covered. Ask which country is more developed and how they know.",
   check:["They use data from the axis, not adjectives.","They can explain a notch or bulge (war, baby boom, policy) rather than ignoring it.","They separate <em>describe</em> from <em>explain</em> when the question splits them."],
   slip:"Saying \"more men than women\" without a figure, or describing the pyramid top-down and running out of time."}},

{code:"1.4",theme:1,title:"Population density and distribution",
 outcomes:["Describe the factors influencing the density and distribution of population"],
 guidance:["Physical, economic, social and political factors"],
 cases:[{t:"A densely populated country or area",src:"Australia (Sydney)",notes:["australia-population-density-and-distribution"]},{t:"A sparsely populated country or area",src:"Australia (Outback)",notes:["australia-population-density-and-distribution"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Do not confuse the two words: density is people per km², distribution is where they are.","Build a factor table with physical (relief, climate, soil, water, resources) against human (jobs, transport, services, government).","Learn one dense and one sparse area with real densities — a figure per km² each.","On a distribution map, describe the overall pattern first, then name two anomalies."],
   q:[["Define the term population density.",2,
        ["The average number of people living in an area (1 mark)…","…measured per square kilometre — people/km² (1 mark). Without the unit you lose the second mark."]],
      ["Describe the distribution of population shown on the map.",4,
        ["Start with the overall pattern — uneven; clustered in some places, sparse in others.","Say where it is dense — coasts, river valleys, lowlands, around named cities — using names from the map.","Say where it is sparse — interior, mountains, deserts.","Quote values from the key, and mention any anomaly.","Describe only; don't explain unless the question asks."]],
      ["Explain why some areas of the world are sparsely populated.",6,
        ["Climate too extreme — too dry (deserts) or too cold — for farming and comfortable living.","Rugged, mountainous relief: hard to build on, farm or connect with transport.","Poor or thin soils, dense vegetation, or a lack of water.","Few jobs, poor transport and services, and remoteness from markets.","Use an example with figures: Australia's Outback gets under 250mm of rain a year (Alice Springs ~180mm) and can exceed 40°C.","Develop each factor: why does it stop people living there?"]]]},
 mentor:{open:"\"Where would you not want to live, and why?\" Turn their answers into physical and human factors on the board.",
   check:["The definition of density includes the unit of area.","They can give a sparse example with a reason — this is the syllabus case study most often missing.","They read distribution maps as patterns (coastal, along rivers, clustered) rather than lists of places."],
   slip:"The sparsely populated example is the one pairs most often skip. Set it as homework and log it here."}},

{code:"1.5",theme:1,title:"Settlements and service provision",
 outcomes:["Explain the patterns of settlement","Describe and explain the factors influencing the sites, growth and functions of settlements","Give reasons for the hierarchy of settlements and services"],
 guidance:["Dispersed, linear and nucleated patterns","Physical factors including relief, soil and water supply; other factors including accessibility and resources","High-, middle- and low-order settlements and services; sphere of influence and threshold population"],
 cases:[{t:"Settlement and service provision in an urban area",src:"Singapore",notes:["singapore-service-provision-in-new-towns"]},{t:"Settlement and service provision in a rural area",src:"Vietnam",notes:["vietnam-rural-settlement-and-service-provision"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Keep site (the land it's built on) and situation (its position relative to other places) firmly apart.","Learn the hierarchy both ways: as population rises, number of settlements falls and services get higher order.","Define range, threshold population and sphere of influence in one sentence each.","Practise identifying dispersed, linear and nucleated patterns from an OS extract."],
   q:[["Define the term sphere of influence.",2,
        ["The area served by a settlement or service (1 mark)…","…i.e. the area people travel in from to use it (1 mark). Also called the market area or hinterland."]],
      ["Describe the site and situation of the settlement shown.",4,
        ["Site: the land the settlement is built on — flat land, dry point above flood level, beside a river, bridging point, defensive hill, sheltered valley.","Situation: its location relative to the surrounding area — near routes, other settlements, the coast, resources.","Use map evidence: heights from contours, names of features, grid references.","Keep site and situation separate."]],
      ["Explain why high-order services are found in fewer settlements.",5,
        ["High-order services (jewellers, department stores, hospitals) sell expensive items people buy rarely.","So they need a large threshold population — the minimum number of customers needed to make a profit.","People are willing to travel further to use them, so they have a large range.","Only large settlements have enough people within reach — and there are few large settlements in the hierarchy.","Contrast: low-order services (newsagent, small grocer) have a small threshold and range, so they appear even in villages."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask where they'd buy milk versus where they'd buy a laptop, then build the low-order/high-order distinction from their answer.",
   check:["Site versus situation — test with a map extract, not a definition.","They can explain threshold, not just define it.","They link wet point and dry point sites to relief and water supply."],
   slip:"Confusing range with sphere of influence. Range is how far a person will travel; sphere of influence is the area served."}},

{code:"1.6",theme:1,title:"Urban settlements",
 outcomes:["Describe and give reasons for the characteristics of, and changes in, land use in urban areas","Explain the problems of urban areas, their causes and possible solutions"],
 guidance:["Land use zones including the CBD, residential, industrial and the rural-urban fringe, in countries at different levels of development","Effects of land use change and rapid urban growth, including urban sprawl","Pollution (air, noise, water, visual), inequality, housing, traffic congestion, conflict over land use"],
 cases:[{t:"An urban area or urban areas",src:"Singapore",notes:["singapore-housing-and-land-use","singapore-managing-traffic-congestion","singapore-light-pollution"]},{t:"Urban sprawl in a city",src:"Sydney",notes:["urban-sprawl-in-sydney"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Learn Burgess and Hoyt as sketches you can draw in 30 seconds, with what each zone is and why.","Tie land use to bid rent: the CBD is expensive because it is most accessible.","For every urban problem, carry a cause, an effect and a named solution — the question almost always wants all three.","Use your city's real schemes and figures, not generic \"build more housing\"."],
   q:[["Describe the characteristics of the CBD of a named city.",4,
        ["Name the city — e.g. Singapore's CBD (Raffles Place / Marina Bay).","Tall, high-density buildings because land values are highest at the centre.","Functions: offices, banks and finance, department stores, hotels, entertainment, government buildings.","Most accessible point — public transport and roads converge there.","Few people live there; very busy in the daytime, with traffic congestion."]],
      ["Explain why traffic congestion occurs in urban areas.",5,
        ["Rising car ownership as people become wealthier.","Many commuters travelling into the centre at the same time (rush hour).","Old, narrow street layouts and limited road space that can't be widened easily.","Inadequate or unattractive public transport, so people drive.","Deliveries, roadworks, accidents and on-street parking block lanes.","Urban sprawl increases journey distances."]],
      ["For a named urban area, describe attempts to reduce one urban problem and evaluate how successful they have been.",7,
        ["Name the city and the problem: traffic congestion in Singapore (around 6 million people in about 700km², ~8,000 people per km²).","Reducing car ownership: the Certificate of Entitlement (COE) — around $100,000 for the right to own a car for 10 years — and a quota keeping growth in vehicle numbers to a small amount each year.","Improving public transport: 6 MRT lines and 4 LRT lines with 140 and 40 stations; more bus routes; cheap fares (Singapore ranked 2nd for affordability).","Evaluate: roads flow well and public-transport use is high, but car ownership is very expensive and peak-hour congestion still occurs.","End with a clear judgement on how successful the measures have been.","7 marks are level-marked: Level 3 (6–7) needs developed points with place-specific detail and an evaluation."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Have them draw Burgess and Hoyt side by side and say what Hoyt added and why.",
   check:["They can explain <em>why</em> land use is zoned (bid rent, accessibility), not just name the zones.","Solutions are named and specific.","They can say where a model does not fit their real city — that's the AO3 mark."],
   slip:"Describing a model as if it were a real city. Models are a starting point; the marks are in the mismatch."}},

{code:"1.7",theme:1,title:"Urbanisation",
 outcomes:["Identify and suggest reasons for rapid urban growth","Describe the impacts of urban growth on rural and urban areas, and possible solutions"],
 guidance:["Physical, economic and social factors causing rural depopulation and movement to major cities","Effects of urbanisation on people and the natural environment","Characteristics of squatter settlements","Strategies to reduce the negative impacts"],
 cases:[{t:"A rapidly growing urban area in a developing country and migration to it",src:"Dharavi, Mumbai",notes:["dharavi-a-rapidly-growing-urban-area"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Separate urbanisation (the proportion living in cities) from urban growth (the number of people).","Learn squatter settlement characteristics as a list you can produce fast: services, materials, density, tenure, work.","Note the effects back in the rural source area — an easy mark most candidates skip.","Know two upgrading strategies for your named city and one limitation of each."],
   q:[["Describe the characteristics of a squatter settlement.",4,
        ["Built illegally on land the residents don't own, often unwanted land — steep slopes, floodplains, beside railways or dumps.","Homes self-built from scrap materials such as corrugated iron, wood and plastic.","Lack of basic services: no piped water, sewerage, electricity or rubbish collection.","Very high density, overcrowded, with narrow unpaved lanes; high risk of disease and fire.","Often a busy informal economy and a strong community."]],
      ["Suggest reasons for rapid urban growth in developing countries.",5,
        ["Rural-to-urban migration — push: poverty, lack of jobs, mechanisation, drought, poor services in the countryside.","Pull: the hope of jobs in factories and the informal sector, better schools and healthcare.","Natural increase: migrants are mostly young, so birth rates in the city are high while healthcare lowers death rates.","Cover both migration and natural increase for full marks."]],
      ["For a named city in a developing country, describe the problems caused by rapid growth and explain attempts to solve them.",7,
        ["Name it with figures: Dharavi, Mumbai — about 2.39km² with an estimated 300,000 to 1 million people (density over 400,000 per km²).","Problems: overcrowding; about one toilet for every 1,440 residents with open sewers into Mahim Creek; monsoon flooding (2005); fire (2013); TB, cholera and dysentery.","Its strength: an informal economy (leather, textiles, pottery, recycling employing ~250,000) worth $500 million to over $1 billion a year.","Attempts: redevelopment plans since 2004 (e.g. Mukesh Mehta's plan for 57,000 families), and the $2.4 billion Adani Group project (tender awarded 2024) for in-situ rehabilitation.","Evaluate: high-rise rehousing can break up communities and home-based workshops, not every resident is eligible, and progress has been slow.","7 marks are level-marked: Level 3 (6–7) needs both parts of the question answered with place-specific detail."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"\"Why does someone leave a farm for a city that has no house waiting for them?\" Push until they reach both push and pull.",
   check:["They give figures for their named city's growth.","Impacts include the rural area, not only the city.","Solutions are the city's actual schemes with a named agency where possible."],
   slip:"Blurring this with 1.2. Migration is the mechanism; urbanisation is the outcome. Keep the case studies distinct."}},

{code:"2.1",theme:2,title:"Earthquakes and volcanoes",
 outcomes:["Describe the main types and features of volcanoes and earthquakes","Describe and explain their distribution","Describe the causes and effects on people and the environment","Understand that volcanoes present hazards and offer opportunities","Explain what can be done to reduce impacts"],
 guidance:["Strato-volcanoes and shield volcanoes; crater, vent, magma chamber; epicentre, focus, magnitude","Global pattern of plates, plate movement and subduction; constructive/divergent, destructive/convergent and conservative boundaries"],
 cases:[{t:"An earthquake",src:"Christchurch 2011",notes:["christchurch-2011-earthquake"]},{t:"A volcano",src:"Eyjafjallajökull 2010",notes:["eyjafjallaj-kull-2010-eruption"]}],
 mentee:{do:["For each boundary type, know: what moves how, whether there are earthquakes, whether there are volcanoes, and <em>why not</em> when there aren't.","Split effects into primary and secondary, and social, economic and environmental — that grid is the answer plan.","Split responses into immediate and long-term.","Learn reduction strategies as prediction, protection and preparation."],
   q:[["Describe the global distribution of earthquakes.",4,
        ["Earthquakes occur in long, narrow belts, mostly along plate boundaries.","The Pacific \"Ring of Fire\" — around the edge of the Pacific Ocean (Japan, Chile, west coast of the USA, New Zealand).","Along mid-ocean ridges, e.g. the Mid-Atlantic Ridge.","A belt from the Mediterranean through to the Himalayas.","Few in the middle of plates. Describe the pattern with named places — no explanation is needed."]],
      ["Explain why volcanoes occur at destructive plate boundaries.",5,
        ["At a destructive boundary a denser oceanic plate moves towards a continental plate and is subducted beneath it.","As it descends into the mantle, heat and friction cause it to melt, forming magma.","The magma is less dense than the surrounding rock, so it rises through cracks in the crust.","It erupts at the surface, building volcanoes — often explosive, steep-sided composite volcanoes because the magma is thick and gas-rich.","A sequenced explanation (or labelled diagram) with a named example, e.g. the Andes, earns the top marks."]],
      ["For a named earthquake, describe its effects and evaluate the responses to it.",7,
        ["Name it with details: Christchurch, New Zealand, 22 February 2011, magnitude 6.3 at a very shallow depth of about 5km.","Primary effects: 185 deaths and over 9,000 injured; around 100,000 properties damaged and 10,000 demolished; liquefaction; cathedral spire collapsed.","Secondary effects: about one-fifth of residents relocated; businesses closed; long-lasting psychological impact; around $28 billion in losses.","Immediate responses: international search-and-rescue teams (over 300 Australian police), temporary sanitation for around 30,000 residents.","Long-term responses: Canterbury Earthquake Recovery Authority (CERA); around 10,000 affordable homes built; stricter building codes for the rebuilt CBD.","Evaluate: a fast, well-funded response from a developed country, but rebuilding took years.","7 marks are level-marked: Level 3 (6–7) needs effects and responses with place-specific detail and an evaluation."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Give them a blank world map and ask them to draw where earthquakes happen. The Pacific ring will appear; ask why it's a ring.",
   check:["They can explain the absence of volcanoes at conservative and collision boundaries.","Haiti figures are attached to effects, not floating.","They know why people still live in hazardous areas — that's the opportunities mark."],
   slip:"Describing distribution as \"everywhere near the sea\". Insist on named plates, belts and continents."}},

{code:"2.2",theme:2,title:"Rivers",
 outcomes:["Explain the hydrological characteristics and processes in rivers and drainage basins","Understand the work of a river in eroding, transporting and depositing","Describe and explain the landforms formed","Understand that rivers present hazards and offer opportunities","Explain how the impacts of river flooding can be managed"],
 guidance:["Width, depth, speed of flow, discharge; watershed, tributary, confluence","Interception, infiltration, throughflow, groundwater flow, evaporation, overland flow","Long profile and cross-section, waterfalls, potholes, meanders, oxbow lakes, deltas, levées, flood plains","Hazards including flooding and river erosion; opportunities of living on a flood plain or delta"],
 cases:[{t:"Opportunities from a river, its hazards and their management",src:"Bangladesh flooding",notes:["bangladesh-river-flooding"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Learn landform formation as a numbered sequence — examiners mark the steps, and each step is a mark.","Name the erosion processes (hydraulic action, abrasion, attrition, solution) and the transport processes (traction, saltation, suspension, solution) and use them by name.","Know how width, depth, velocity and discharge change downstream, and why velocity rises despite the gentler gradient.","Keep hard and soft engineering separate, each with an advantage and a disadvantage."],
   q:[["Describe how a waterfall is formed.",5,
        ["Forms where a band of hard (resistant) rock lies on top of soft (less resistant) rock.","The soft rock is eroded faster by hydraulic action and abrasion, creating a step.","Water plunges over the edge and erodes a plunge pool at the base.","The soft rock is undercut, leaving an overhang of hard rock that eventually collapses.","The process repeats, so the waterfall retreats upstream, leaving a steep-sided gorge.","Use the process names; a labelled sequence of diagrams can earn the marks too."]],
      ["Explain why flooding occurs on some rivers.",6,
        ["Heavy or prolonged rainfall (e.g. monsoon), or rapid snow and glacier melt.","Saturated, frozen or impermeable ground, so water runs off instead of soaking in.","Steep slopes and a small, circular drainage basin speed up runoff.","Deforestation reduces interception and infiltration, so more surface runoff reaches the river faster.","Urbanisation: concrete and tarmac are impermeable, and drains carry water quickly to the river.","Link each factor to more or faster water reaching the river, until it exceeds bankfull capacity."]],
      ["For a named river, describe the flood management strategies used and evaluate their success.",7,
        ["Name it with figures: Bangladesh, on the Ganges delta — 170 million people, 156 million of them living on floodplains.","Causes in brief: monsoon rain, Himalayan glacier melt, low-lying delta, deforestation upstream.","Effects to set the scene: 1.5 million people forced from their homes and 200,000 tons of vegetables destroyed each year.","Strategies: embankments along rivers, raised flood shelters and homes on plinths, flood-warning systems, tree planting upstream.","Evaluate each: embankments are expensive and can fail; shelters and warnings save lives cheaply; cooperation with India and Nepal is needed.","7 marks are level-marked: Level 3 (6–7) needs strategies with place-specific detail and an evaluation."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask them to draw the long profile and mark where each landform belongs. Misplaced landforms show up immediately.",
   check:["Oxbow lake and meander formation are in sequence, with erosion and deposition on the correct banks.","They can state the opportunities of living on a flood plain — the case study wants both sides.","They distinguish a river's discharge from its speed."],
   slip:"\"Erosion happens\" as a step. Name which process, on which bank, and what it produces."}},

{code:"2.3",theme:2,title:"Coasts",
 outcomes:["Understand the work of the sea and wind in eroding, transporting and depositing","Describe and explain the formation of coastal landforms","Describe coral reefs and mangrove swamps and the conditions they need","Understand that coasts present hazards and offer opportunities","Explain how the impacts of coastal erosion can be managed"],
 guidance:["Cliffs, wave-cut platforms, caves, arches, stacks, stumps, bays and headlands, beaches, spits, coastal sand dunes","Hazards including coastal erosion and tropical storms"],
 cases:[{t:"Opportunities from a coastline, its hazards and their management",src:"Gold Coast, Australia",notes:["mangroves-and-coastal-management-gold-coast"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Constructive versus destructive waves: compare swash and backwash, height, frequency and what each does to a beach.","Learn longshore drift as a labelled sequence — it explains spits, and spits are asked about often.","For coral and mangroves, learn the growth conditions as numbers: temperature, depth, salinity, light.","Learn the management table by strategy, advantage, disadvantage and named example."],
   q:[["Describe the conditions needed for coral reefs to grow.",4,
        ["Warm sea water — above about 18°C (ideally 23–25°C).","Shallow, clear water so sunlight reaches the algae living in the coral, which need it to photosynthesise.","Clean water with little sediment — away from river mouths and pollution.","Salt water; fresh water from rivers kills coral.","Each condition needs a detail or reason for the mark."]],
      ["Explain the formation of a spit.",5,
        ["Longshore drift moves sediment along the coast — swash at an angle in the direction of the prevailing wind, backwash straight down the beach.","Where the coastline changes direction (e.g. at a river mouth or bay), sediment keeps being carried out into the sea.","Energy falls and the sediment is deposited, building a ridge of sand or shingle outwards.","Changes in wind and wave direction curve the end into a hook (recurved end).","Sheltered water behind the spit can become salt marsh. A labelled diagram helps."]],
      ["For a named coastline, evaluate the strategies used to manage coastal erosion.",7,
        ["Name it with figures: the Gold Coast, Queensland, Australia — about 57km of sandy beaches and 12 million visitors a year.","Hazards: erosion by waves and longshore drift (made worse by the Tweed River training walls), cyclones such as Dinah (1967) and Hamish (2009) removing 8 million m³ of sand.","Tweed Sand Bypassing Project: pumps sand under the river mouth to restore longshore drift — 500,000m³ moved since 2000.","Beach nourishment: sand dredged from creeks, e.g. Currumbin to Palm Beach — 3.5 million m³ added 1995–2000.","Seawalls such as Kurrawa (2016): protect property, but can narrow the beach; dune fencing and brush matting stabilise dunes.","Evaluate each strategy (cost, effectiveness, side-effects) and reach an overall judgement.","7 marks are level-marked: Level 3 (6–7) needs place-specific detail and an evaluation."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"\"Why is there sand at one end of the bay and not the other?\" Longshore drift usually emerges from this without prompting.",
   check:["Wave refraction is used to explain why headlands erode and bays fill.","They can name a real scheme, not just \"a sea wall\".","Coral conditions are quantified."],
   slip:"Formation answers that jump from \"crack\" to \"stack\". Insist on cave, arch, stack, stump in order with the process at each stage."}},

{code:"2.4",theme:2,title:"Weather",
 outcomes:["Describe how weather data are collected","Make calculations using information from weather instruments","Use and interpret graphs and diagrams showing weather and climate data"],
 guidance:["The characteristics, siting and use of a Stevenson Screen","Rain gauge, maximum-minimum thermometer, wet-and-dry bulb hygrometer, sunshine recorder, barometer, anemometer, wind vane, and digital equivalents; cloud type and amount"],
 cases:[],
 mentee:{do:["For each instrument: what it measures, the unit, and one siting rule with the reason.","Know the Stevenson Screen in detail — white, louvred, 1.25 m, door facing away from the sun, over grass — and why each feature exists.","Practise the calculations: mean, range, and relative humidity from wet and dry bulb readings.","Learn cloud cover in oktas and the main cloud types by height and shape."],
   q:[["Describe how rainfall is measured accurately.",4,
        ["Use a rain gauge: a funnel feeding into a collecting container inside an outer cylinder.","Place it in the open, away from buildings and trees that would shelter it or drip into it.","Sink it into the ground with the rim about 30cm above the surface, so rain doesn't splash in and it can't be knocked over.","Read it at the same time every day: pour into a measuring cylinder, read at eye level, record in mm, then empty it."]],
      ["Explain why a Stevenson Screen is painted white and has louvred sides.",4,
        ["White reflects the sun's rays, so the screen doesn't heat up…","…meaning the thermometers record the true shade air temperature, not the heat of direct sunlight.","Louvred (slatted) sides let air circulate freely through the screen…","…while keeping out direct sunlight and rain."]],
      ["Calculate the mean monthly temperature and the annual range from the table.",3,
        ["Mean monthly temperature: add all 12 monthly figures and divide by 12.","Annual range: highest monthly temperature minus the lowest.","Show your working, and include the unit (°C) — a missing unit can cost the mark."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Point at anything outside and ask how they'd measure it properly. Siting errors are where the marks are.",
   check:["Every siting rule comes with a reason.","They can convert oktas and read a climate graph off two different axes.","They give units. Every time."],
   slip:"This topic is heavily assessed on Paper 2 and thinly revised. Give it a session of its own even though it looks small."}},

{code:"2.5",theme:2,title:"Climate and natural vegetation",
 outcomes:["Describe and explain the characteristics of equatorial and hot desert climates","Describe and explain tropical rainforest and hot desert ecosystems","Describe the causes and effects of deforestation of tropical rainforest"],
 guidance:["Temperature (hottest month, coolest month, annual range) and precipitation including convectional and relief rainfall","Latitude, pressure systems, winds, distance from the sea, altitude and ocean currents","The relationship between natural vegetation, soil, wildlife and climate","Effects of deforestation locally and globally, and on people"],
 cases:[{t:"An area of tropical rainforest",src:"Heart of Borneo",notes:["heart-of-borneo-rainforest"]},{t:"An area of hot desert",src:"Mojave",notes:["mojave-desert"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Learn both climate graphs by their numbers: hottest month, coolest month, annual range, total rainfall, seasonality.","Explain the climate through the factors — latitude and low pressure for the equatorial belt, subtropical high pressure for deserts.","Learn plant and animal adaptations as feature → purpose pairs.","For deforestation, split causes from effects and effects into local, global and human."],
   q:[["Describe the characteristics of an equatorial climate.",4,
        ["Hot all year, about 26–28°C (Borneo averages ~27°C).","Very small annual temperature range — only 2–3°C.","High rainfall, over 2,000mm a year (Borneo 2,500–3,000mm), spread through every month with no dry season.","Daily convectional rain, often heavy afternoon thunderstorms; high humidity.","Quote figures from a climate graph if one is given."]],
      ["Explain how plants are adapted to survive in a hot desert.",5,
        ["Long roots reach deep groundwater, or wide shallow roots collect rain quickly over a large area.","Fleshy stems store water (succulents such as cacti).","Spines, small leaves and waxy coatings reduce water loss by transpiration (spines also deter animals).","Seeds lie dormant for years, then germinate and flower quickly after rain.","Stomata open at night (CAM photosynthesis) to cut water loss in the heat of the day.","Use named Mojave plants: Joshua tree, creosote bush, barrel cactus. Link each adaptation to how it saves or finds water."]],
      ["For an area of tropical rainforest, explain the causes of deforestation and describe its effects.",7,
        ["Name it: the Heart of Borneo, shared by Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei — about 50% of the rainforest cleared since the 1980s.","Causes: palm-oil plantations, logging for timber (teak, mahogany), mining and oil extraction, hydroelectric dams, road building.","Effects on the environment: loss of habitat and biodiversity (orangutans, pygmy elephants); soil erosion and leaching once the canopy is gone; flooding and silting of rivers.","Effects on climate: carbon released and less carbon dioxide absorbed.","Effects on people: indigenous communities lose land and their way of life — but there are jobs and export income.","7 marks are level-marked: Level 3 (6–7) needs both causes and effects with place-specific detail."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Put the two climate graphs up unlabelled. Ask which is which and what gives it away.",
   check:["They explain rainfall by convection and pressure, not just \"it's hot\".","Adaptations are paired with a purpose.","The nutrient cycle is understood — why cleared rainforest soil fails quickly."],
   slip:"Describing the rainforest layers without linking them to competition for light. The link is the explanation mark."}},

{code:"3.1",theme:3,title:"Development",
 outcomes:["Use a variety of indicators to assess a country's level of development","Identify and explain inequalities between and within countries","Classify production into sectors and give illustrations","Describe and explain how employment structure varies with development","Describe and explain globalisation and consider its impacts"],
 guidance:["GNP per capita, literacy, life expectancy and composite indices such as HDI","Primary, secondary, tertiary and quaternary sectors","Technology and transnational corporations in globalisation; impacts at local, national and global scale"],
 cases:[{t:"A transnational corporation and its global links",src:"Nike in Vietnam",notes:["nike-factories-in-vietnam"]}],
 mentee:{do:["For each indicator, be ready with one strength and one weakness — GDP per capita hides inequality, and so on.","Know why composite indices like HDI are preferred, and which three components they combine.","Read triangular graphs correctly: practise until you can plot and read a point in under a minute.","For your TNC, know its benefits and costs for the host country separately."],
   q:[["Explain why GNP per capita alone is not a good measure of development.",4,
        ["It is an average, so it hides inequality between rich and poor and between regions.","It ignores the informal economy and subsistence farming, which produce no recorded income.","It doesn't measure quality of life — health, education, housing, environment, freedom.","It doesn't allow for differences in the cost of living between countries.","Better to use a composite measure such as HDI (life expectancy, education and income)."]],
      ["Describe how employment structure changes as a country develops.",5,
        ["In the least developed countries most people work in the primary sector — farming, fishing, mining.","As a country industrialises, the secondary (manufacturing) sector grows as people move from farms to factories.","In developed countries the tertiary (services) sector dominates and the quaternary (research, IT) sector grows.","Primary employment falls throughout because of mechanisation; secondary later falls as manufacturing moves abroad or is automated.","Quote percentages if a triangular graph or table is provided."]],
      ["For a named transnational corporation, evaluate its impacts on the host country.",7,
        ["Name it with figures: Nike, a US sportswear TNC based in Oregon — 105 factories in Vietnam with around 460,000 workers, 80% of them female.","Positive: jobs for a large, young workforce, foreign investment, exports, infrastructure around Ho Chi Minh City's industrial zones, skills.","Negative: very low wages (around $1.25 a day), long hours and poor working conditions.","Negative: most profits go back to the USA, and the factories could relocate to cheaper countries.","Negative: reliance on one company, and pollution from industry.","Weigh up the positives and negatives and reach a judgement for the host country.","7 marks are level-marked: Level 3 (6–7) needs place-specific detail and an evaluation."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask them to rank four countries with only GDP per capita, then reveal life expectancy and literacy. The ranking usually shifts — that's the lesson.",
   check:["Triangular graph reading — set two points and check.","They can give a within-country inequality example, not just between countries.","TNC impacts include the negatives: wages, conditions, profit repatriation."],
   slip:"Sector definitions given without examples. Ask for two examples each, including quaternary."}},

{code:"3.2",theme:3,title:"Food production",
 outcomes:["Describe and explain the main features of an agricultural system: inputs, processes and outputs","Recognise the causes and effects of food shortages and describe possible solutions"],
 guidance:["Commercial and subsistence; arable, pastoral and mixed; intensive and extensive","Natural inputs (relief, climate, soil) and human inputs (economic and social) and their influence on scale, organisation and products","Natural causes (drought, floods, tropical storms, pests) and economic and political causes (low investment, poor distribution, war)","Effects of food shortages, food aid, and measures to increase output"],
 cases:[{t:"A farm or agricultural system",src:"Hill sheep farming, Lake District",notes:["hill-sheep-farming-lake-district"]},{t:"A country or region suffering from food shortages",src:"South Sudan",notes:["south-sudan-food-shortages"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Draw your named farm as a systems diagram: inputs, processes, outputs — and use its real figures.","Keep the farming type pairs apart: commercial/subsistence, arable/pastoral, intensive/extensive.","Split food shortage causes into physical and human before you write.","Know the solutions — irrigation, HYVs, mechanisation, aid — with a drawback each."],
   q:[["Describe the inputs of a named farming system.",4,
        ["Name it: hill sheep farming in the Lake District, Cumbria.","Physical inputs: steep slopes, poor acidic soils, over 2,000mm of rain a year, short growing season (~150 days), average 5–8°C.","Human inputs: hardy sheep breeds (Herdwick, Swaledale), family labour, winter feed (hay or silage), government subsidies (ELMs).","Keep physical and human inputs separate; inputs only (not processes or outputs)."]],
      ["Explain the physical causes of food shortages.",5,
        ["Drought or unreliable rainfall: crops fail and livestock die.","Floods and tropical storms destroy crops and drown animals.","Pests and diseases, e.g. locusts, wipe out harvests.","Soil erosion, soil exhaustion and desertification reduce yields.","Extreme temperatures and climate change shorten growing seasons.","Physical causes only — explain how each one cuts food production."]],
      ["For a named country or region, describe the effects of food shortages and evaluate attempts to increase food supply.",7,
        ["Name it with figures: South Sudan — independent since 2011, 12.6 million people, one of the most food-insecure countries in the world.","Effects: 40% of the population (4 million people) face acute food shortages; 70,000+ deaths from hunger and disease during the war; high child malnutrition and mortality; farming and the economy collapse.","Attempts: UN World Food Programme emergency aid; Red Cross air drops; FAO projects teaching watermelon farming and fish preservation to women; a focus on female education and local resilience.","Evaluate: aid saves lives in the short term but is often disrupted by violence; long-term food supply depends on peace and rebuilding infrastructure.","7 marks are level-marked: Level 3 (6–7) needs effects and attempts with place-specific detail and an evaluation."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"\"Turn your farm into a flow diagram in two minutes.\" What they leave out of processes is what they don't understand.",
   check:["Inputs are split into physical and human.","They don't confuse processes with outputs.","The food shortage case study has dates, numbers of people affected, and named agencies."],
   slip:"Answering \"food shortage\" only as drought. Conflict and distribution failure carry equal weight in this syllabus."}},

{code:"3.3",theme:3,title:"Industry",
 outcomes:["Understand an industrial system: inputs, processes and outputs including waste","Describe and explain the factors influencing the distribution and location of factories and industrial zones"],
 guidance:["Manufacturing, processing, assembly and high technology industry","Land, labour, raw materials, fuel and power, transport, markets and political factors","Industrial zones and factories with respect to locational and siting factors"],
 cases:[{t:"An industrial zone or factory",src:"Bangalore, India",notes:["bangalore-industrial-zone"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Use the same systems structure as farming: inputs, processes, outputs — and remember waste is an output.","Separate <em>location</em> (which region or country) from <em>site</em> (which exact plot, and why there).","Learn why high-technology industry locates differently: skilled labour, universities, airports, environment.","For your factory, be able to give three specific location factors with evidence."],
   q:[["Describe the inputs and outputs of a named industry.",4,
        ["Name the industry — e.g. IT and software in Bangalore.","Inputs: skilled graduates, computers and equipment, energy, fast internet, capital, land in technology parks.","Processes: software development, research, data processing.","Outputs: software and IT services (largely exported), profit (some reinvested), waste.","Make the inputs and outputs specific to the named industry."]],
      ["Explain the factors that influence the location of high technology industry.",5,
        ["Near universities and research centres, for skilled graduates and research links.","Good transport — motorways and international airports for staff and light, high-value products.","Pleasant environment and science parks on city edges attract skilled workers; land is cheaper there.","Clustering: firms share ideas, suppliers and workers (agglomeration).","Government incentives; the industry is footloose because it doesn't depend on heavy raw materials."]],
      ["For a named industrial zone, explain why it developed in that location.",7,
        ["Name it: Bangalore (Bengaluru), Karnataka — the \"Silicon Valley of India\", with zones such as Electronics City and Whitefield.","Skilled labour: over 85,000 engineering graduates a year from institutions such as the Indian Institute of Science.","Government support: Karnataka IT Policy, Software Technology Parks (STPI), dedicated zones.","Cost advantage: cheaper labour and running costs than Mumbai or Western cities, attractive to outsourcing firms and TNCs.","Accessibility: well connected, an expanding metro, close to the Asia-Pacific region; clustering of firms such as Infosys, Wipro, IBM and Intel.","Develop each factor — why does it attract firms to this location?","7 marks are level-marked: Level 3 (6–7) needs developed reasons with place-specific detail."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"\"Why does Nike make shoes there rather than here?\" Let them build the factor list themselves, then name each factor formally.",
   check:["Waste is listed as an output.","They can distinguish site from situation factors for industry.","Political factors — tax, tariffs, regulation — appear, not just cheap labour."],
   slip:"One-word factors. \"Labour\" is not a mark; \"a large supply of low-cost semi-skilled labour\" is."}},

{code:"3.4",theme:3,title:"Tourism",
 outcomes:["Describe and explain the growth of tourism in relation to physical and human attractions","Evaluate the benefits and disadvantages of tourism to receiving areas","Understand that tourism must be carefully managed to be sustainable"],
 guidance:[],
 cases:[{t:"An area where tourism is important",src:"Gold Coast, Australia",notes:["gold-coast-tourism"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Split attractions into physical and human, and reasons for growth into transport, income, marketing, holidays and package travel.","Group impacts as economic, social and environmental, each with positives and negatives.","Learn multiplier effect and leakage — both are worth marks in evaluation answers.","Know what makes tourism sustainable and one real scheme that does it."],
   q:[["Suggest reasons for the growth of international tourism.",5,
        ["Rising incomes and paid holidays mean more people can afford to travel.","Cheaper, faster air travel (budget airlines, larger aircraft).","The internet: online booking, cheap deals, information and social media.","More leisure time and early retirement, with a wealthy older population.","Package holidays and marketing open up new destinations.","Develop each reason — say why it leads to more international tourists."]],
      ["Describe the disadvantages of tourism for a receiving area.",4,
        ["Environmental damage: footpath erosion, litter, pollution, damage to coral reefs and habitats.","Pressure on water and energy supplies; traffic congestion.","Jobs are often seasonal and low-paid; profits leak to foreign companies.","Higher prices and house prices for local people.","Loss of local culture and traditions; crime or conflict with residents."]],
      ["For a named area, evaluate attempts to make tourism more sustainable.",7,
        ["Name the area and the problems tourism causes there — e.g. the Gold Coast: erosion, habitat loss, waste, overdevelopment, traffic.","Attempts: beach nourishment and erosion control (Palm Beach), eco-tourism in hinterland national parks (Lamington, Springbrook), sustainable planning for new developments, light rail to cut traffic.","Evaluate each: who benefits, how effective it is, and whether it conflicts with growth (12 million visitors a year; over 42,000 jobs).","Finish with a judgement on how sustainable tourism there has become.","7 marks are level-marked: Level 3 (6–7) needs place-specific detail and an evaluation."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask what their named area would lose if tourists stopped coming tomorrow — and what it would gain.",
   check:["Both sides of every impact appear.","They can define sustainable tourism rather than just praising it.","Figures for visitor numbers and tourism income are attached to the case study."],
   slip:"Confusing ecotourism with any outdoor tourism. Ecotourism is small-scale, low-impact and returns money locally."}},

{code:"3.5",theme:3,title:"Energy",
 outcomes:["Describe the importance of non-renewable fossil fuels, renewable energy, nuclear power and fuelwood, globally and in countries at different levels of development","Evaluate the benefits and disadvantages of nuclear power and renewable energy"],
 guidance:["Coal, oil and natural gas","Geothermal, wind, HEP, wave and tidal, solar and biofuels"],
 cases:[{t:"Energy supply in a country or area",src:"Singapore",notes:["singapore-energy-supply"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Build one table: each source with two advantages, two disadvantages and where it's used.","Explain <em>why</em> energy mix differs with development — cost, technology, resources, politics.","Nuclear needs a balanced answer: reliability and low carbon against waste, cost and accident risk.","Know why your named country's mix is what it is — its geography usually explains it."],
   q:[["Describe the advantages of using renewable energy.",4,
        ["Renewable sources will not run out — they are sustainable.","Little or no greenhouse gas is released while generating, so they reduce climate change and air pollution.","Low running costs once built, because the \"fuel\" (wind, sunlight, water) is free.","Energy security: less reliance on imported fossil fuels.","New jobs, and small-scale schemes (e.g. solar) can supply remote areas."]],
      ["Explain why energy consumption varies between countries at different levels of development.",5,
        ["Developed countries use much more energy per person: industry, many vehicles, heating and air-conditioning, appliances.","Developing countries use little: many people live rurally, rely on fuelwood and lack access to the electricity grid.","Emerging countries' use rises quickly with industrialisation, urbanisation and rising incomes.","Climate and local resources also matter — heating in cold countries, cooling in hot ones.","Explain the link between level of development and the amount of energy used."]],
      ["Evaluate the benefits and disadvantages of nuclear power.",7,
        ["Benefits: very low carbon emissions during generation, helping to tackle climate change.","Benefits: a tiny amount of uranium produces a huge amount of electricity; reliable whatever the weather; takes up little land.","Disadvantages: radioactive waste stays dangerous for thousands of years and is hard to store safely.","Disadvantages: risk of serious accidents (Chernobyl 1986, Fukushima 2011); very expensive to build and decommission; long construction times.","Disadvantages: security risks, and uranium is a finite resource.","Reach a judgement — do the benefits outweigh the risks?","7 marks are level-marked: Level 3 (6–7) needs developed points on both sides and an evaluation."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"\"Why can't Singapore run on hydroelectric power?\" Physical constraints on energy choice land quickly through this.",
   check:["They distinguish energy production from energy consumption.","Renewables get honest disadvantages — intermittency, land use, cost.","Fuelwood appears — it's in the syllabus and almost always forgotten."],
   slip:"Treating \"renewable\" and \"clean\" as identical. Ask about HEP dams and biofuel land use."}},

{code:"3.6",theme:3,title:"Water",
 outcomes:["Describe methods of water supply and the proportions used for agriculture, domestic and industrial purposes at different levels of development","Explain why water shortages occur and demonstrate that careful management is needed"],
 guidance:["Reservoirs and dams, wells and bore holes, desalination","The impact of lack of clean water on people and on the potential for economic development"],
 cases:[{t:"Water supply in a country or area",src:"Singapore",notes:["singapore-water-supply"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Know the global split of water use — agriculture dominates — and how it shifts with development.","Separate physical water scarcity from economic water scarcity, with an example of each.","For each supply method, give one advantage and one disadvantage.","Link dirty water to specific diseases and to lost school and work days, not just \"illness\"."],
   q:[["Describe the methods used to supply water in a named country.",4,
        ["Name it: Singapore's \"four national taps\".","Imported water: about 250 million gallons a day from Malaysia's Johor River under the 1962 agreement.","Local catchment: rain collected through drains and canals into 17 reservoirs (e.g. Marina Barrage); two-thirds of the land is water catchment.","Desalination: 5 plants using reverse osmosis (e.g. Keppel Marina East, ~30 million gallons a day).","NEWater: used water recycled to drinking quality at 4 plants with advanced membranes and UV disinfection."]],
      ["Explain the difference between physical and economic water scarcity.",4,
        ["Physical water scarcity: there isn't enough water to meet demand because of a natural shortage — low rainfall, arid climate, over-use.","Economic water scarcity: water exists, but people can't get it because of a lack of money and infrastructure — pipes, wells, treatment.","Give an example of each, e.g. the Middle East (physical) and much of sub-Saharan Africa (economic)."]],
      ["Explain the impacts of a lack of clean water on people and on development.",6,
        ["Health: water-borne diseases (cholera, typhoid, diarrhoea) cause deaths, especially of children.","Sick people can't work or go to school, reducing incomes and education.","Time: women and children spend hours collecting water instead of studying or earning.","Farming: without water for irrigation crops fail, causing food shortages and poverty.","Industry needs water, so a shortage limits economic growth; governments spend more on healthcare — holding back development.","Cover impacts on both people and development, and link them."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"Ask where their own tap water comes from, step by step. Most can't get past \"the tap\" — that's the entry point.",
   check:["Physical versus economic scarcity is clear with examples.","They mention agriculture as the largest user.","Impacts are specific: named diseases, lost income, time collecting water."],
   slip:"Answering water shortage as only rainfall. Population growth, pollution and infrastructure are equally examinable."}},

{code:"3.7",theme:3,title:"Environmental risks of economic development",
 outcomes:["Describe how economic activities threaten the natural environment and people, locally and globally","Demonstrate the need for sustainable development and management","Understand the importance of resource conservation"],
 guidance:["Soil erosion, desertification, enhanced global warming, and pollution of water, air, noise and visual"],
 cases:[{t:"An area where economic development is putting the environment at risk",src:"Citarum River, Indonesia",notes:["citarum-river-pollution"]}],
 mentee:{do:["Learn the mechanism of the enhanced greenhouse effect, not just its name.","For soil erosion and desertification: causes, effects, and prevention strategies as three separate lists.","Sort pollution by type — water, air, noise, visual — and give a source and an effect for each.","Define sustainable development properly and apply it to a real scheme."],
   q:[["Describe the causes of soil erosion.",4,
        ["Deforestation removes the roots that bind soil and the canopy that intercepts rain, so rain washes soil away.","Overgrazing strips the vegetation cover from the soil.","Over-cultivation and monoculture exhaust the soil, leaving it dry and easily eroded.","Ploughing up and down slopes creates channels for runoff.","Wind blows away bare, dry soil; heavy rain on steep slopes washes it downhill."]],
      ["Explain how economic development can damage the natural environment.",6,
        ["Industry: air pollution (carbon dioxide; sulphur dioxide causing acid rain) and untreated waste dumped into rivers.","Agriculture: deforestation, and fertiliser runoff causing eutrophication in rivers and lakes.","Mining and quarrying: landscape scars, habitat loss, toxic waste.","Energy: burning fossil fuels contributes to climate change; dams flood valleys.","Urbanisation and transport: habitat loss and vehicle emissions.","Develop each: economic activity → what it releases or destroys → the effect on the environment."]],
      ["For a named area, describe the environmental damage caused by economic activity and evaluate attempts to manage it.",7,
        ["Name it with figures: the Citarum River, West Java, Indonesia — it supplies water, irrigation and hydropower to 28 million people.","Damage: around 2,000 textile factories dump dyes and chemicals, often through illegal \"ghost drains\" — 20,000 tons of solid waste and 340,000 tons of sewage water.","Damage: agricultural fertiliser and pesticide runoff causes eutrophication; up to 90% of livestock waste goes into the river; dense population adds household waste and plastics.","Impacts: people forced to drink and wash in polluted water — over 50,000 deaths linked to poor sanitation; fish populations down 60% since 2008; rice farming in West Java harmed.","Management (not yet in your notes): the government's Citarum Harum programme, launched in 2018, deployed soldiers to clear waste and inspect factories, aiming to make the water drinkable by 2025.","Evaluate: some stretches have improved, but illegal ghost drains make enforcement difficult and the target was very ambitious.","7 marks are level-marked: Level 3 (6–7) needs place-specific detail and an evaluation."]]]},
 mentor:{open:"\"Name the last thing you bought. Where did its damage go?\" It reliably reaches global links.",
   check:["The greenhouse mechanism is explained in steps.","They can define sustainable development in their own words.","The case study includes management attempts and how well they worked."],
   slip:"This topic collects marks from every other one. Ask them to link it back to industry, farming, tourism and energy."}}
];
